Lesson 3 said smaller structs touch fewer cache lines. Here is what that means with numbers. A particle system stores one million of these, 64 bytes each (6 x 4 + 4 + 36, no padding), and one loop moves every particle one unit to the right:

```
struct particle { float x, y, z, vx, vy, vz; int id; char name[36]; };
for (i = 0; i < N; i++) p[i].x += 1.0f;
```

The loop uses 4 bytes per particle. Recall the model: the cache moves memory in aligned lines, 64 bytes in our simplified model, so touching one byte costs its whole line.

